+REAL MODE: High-Frame-Rate Temporal Phase Extrapolator (v2)
+Estimates edge derivatives (velocity, acceleration, jerk, snap) from
+high-rate samples and extrapolates a short horizon ahead, with an error
+estimate measured from the data itself (walk-forward backtest).
+
+What v2 does differently from v1, and why:
+- Time is centered on the last sample and scaled by the window span, so the
+  least-squares fit is well conditioned (cond ~2e1-3e4 instead of ~3e13).
+  Derivatives are rescaled back by span**k.
+- Window length and polynomial degree are chosen by walk-forward
+  cross-validation at the requested horizon, not fixed at 16 / 4.
+- The forecast is the fitted polynomial evaluated ahead. A Taylor series of a
+  degree-d polynomial, truncated at order d, IS that polynomial, so v1's
+  "Taylor step" added nothing; jerk and snap carry no extra information.
+- x_now is the smoothed fit value p(t_now), not the raw (noisy) last sample.
+- The made-up confidence 0.95*exp(-dt/0.05) (floored at 0.5) is gone. v2
+  reports a backtest RMSE and an empirical prediction interval, and says
+  "UNRELIABLE" when the horizon is longer than the backtest can check.
+- Frame rate is computed from the data; uneven sampling and gaps are flagged.
+
+Limits: a polynomial cannot know about dynamics it has not seen (e.g. an
+upcoming snap-through). Backtest windows overlap, so errors are correlated
+and the interval is approximate. Picking the best of many models on the same
+backtest makes its error look slightly better than it is.
+
+Dependencies: numpy only.
